5, -0.0286712646484375, 0.0033588409423828125, -0.0192413330078125, -0.008026123046875, 0.032379150390625, 0.0210723876953125, -0.0009746551513671875, 0.00540924072265625, 0.003154754638671875, 0.0179290771484375, 0.01357269287109375, -0.032257080078125, -0.01885986328125, 0.00980377197265625, 0.00609588623046875, 0.0089111328125, -0.01200103759765625, -0.0096282958984375, -0.02691650390625, -0.0027618408203125, 0.006618499755859375, 0.000598907470703125, -0.004322052001953125, 0.016357421875, -0.0215911865234375, 0.0032253265380859375, -0.0181732177734375, 0.0010900497436523438, -0.011505126953125, 0.029693603515625, 0.02923583984375, 0.032501220703125, -0.01458740234375, 0.0168304443359375, 0.0457763671875, -0.005367279052734375, 0.012603759765625, -0.0306549072265625, 0.019775390625, 0.017791748046875, -0.052581787109375, 0.0130615234375, -0.0096435546875, 0.01255035400390625, 0.0235137939453125, -0.024017333984375, 0.0038394927978515625, 0.011962890625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "**1. Introduction and Related Work**\n\nDistrict heating networks are critical for decarbonizing urban heat supply, but their efficient operation is challenged by the integration of heterogeneous, time-varying renewable sources, the need for thermal storage, and increasingly complex, meshed network topologies. Existing optimization approaches\u2014including mixed-integer linear programming [1], convex relaxations [2], and heuristic methods [3]\u2014struggle with the nonlinearities and combinatorial complexity inherent in these systems, especially for meshed networks, multiple time steps, and realistic operational constraints. Most prior work is limited to tree-like networks, linearized models, or small-scale instances, and does not address the full nonlinear, meshed, multi-supplier, and storage-coupled setting.\n\n**Novelty and Comparison with Prior Work**\n\nOur work introduces the first global optimization framework for stationary nonlinear district heating networks with general (meshed) topologies, multiple suppliers, and thermal storage, capturing nonlinear heat losses and flow-temperature coupling. In contrast to [1], which is restricted to linearized or tree-like networks, and [2], which applies convex relaxations that do not scale to meshed topologies, our approach directly handles nonlinearities and cycles. Unlike [3], which uses heuristics without optimality guarantees, we provide a spatial branch-and-bound algorithm with explicit cycle elimination, symmetry exploitation, and constraint reduction.\n\nTable 1 summarizes the key differences. For each feature, \"\u2713\" indicates full support, \"\u2713 (approximate)\" indicates partial or approximate support, and \"\u2717\" indicates lack of support. For example, [2] supports nonlinear heat loss only via approximations, and [3] supports meshed networks but without guarantees. Our approach is the only one to provide global optimality guarantees for nonlinear, meshed, multi-supplier networks with storage and explicit cycle handling. Recent works such as [8,9] have addressed some aspects (e.g., improved heuristics or convex relaxations for small cycles), but none provide a unified, globally optimal framework for the full problem class.\n\n| Feature                        | [1] MILP | [2] Convex Relaxation | [3] Heuristic | This Work |\n|--------------------------------|----------|----------------------|---------------|-----------|\n| Meshed networks                | \u2717        | \u2717                    | \u2713             | \u2713         |\n| Nonlinear heat loss            | \u2717        | \u2713 (approximate)      | \u2713             | \u2713         |\n| Multiple suppliers/storage     | \u2717        | \u2717                    | \u2713             | \u2713         |\n| Global optimality guarantees   | \u2713        | \u2713 (relaxed)          | \u2717             | \u2713         |\n| Explicit cycle elimination     | \u2717        | \u2717                    | \u2717             | \u2713         |\n| Symmetry exploitation          | \u2717        | \u2717                    | \u2717             | \u2713         |\n| Constraint reduction           | \u2717        | \u2717                    | \u2717             | \u2713         |\n| Primal heuristics              | \u2717        | \u2717                    | \u2713             | \u2713         |\n\n**2. Precise Problem Statement**\n\nWe consider the following stationary optimization problem for a district heating network over a single time step.\n\n**Sets and Definitions:**\n- \\( V \\): set of nodes, including all junctions, consumers, suppliers, storage units, and mixing nodes.\n- \\( E \\subseteq V \\times V \\): set of directed edges (pipes) in the network graph \\( G = (V, E) \\).\n- \\( S \\subseteq V \\): set of supplier nodes, where heat is injected into the network.\n- \\( D \\subseteq V \\): set of demand (consumer) nodes, where heat is withdrawn.\n- \\( M \\subseteq V \\): set of mixing nodes, defined as nodes with multiple incoming edges where perfect mixing is assumed.\n- \\( \\mathcal{C} \\): set of fundamental cycles in \\( G \\), i.e., a basis for the cycle space of the graph, constructed via Paton's algorithm [10]. Each cycle \\( C \\in \\mathcal{C} \\) is an ordered sequence of edges forming a closed path.\n- \\( V_{stor} \\subseteq V \\): set of storage nodes, i.e., nodes equipped with thermal storage units.\n\n**Parameters:**\n- \\( d_i \\): net demand at node \\( i \\) (\\( >0 \\) for consumers, \\( <0 \\) for suppliers, \\( =0 \\) for other nodes).\n- \\( C_i(x_i) \\): cost function for supplier \\( i \\).\n- \\( \\alpha_{ik} \\): heat loss coefficient for pipe \\( (i,k) \\).\n- \\( L_{ik} \\): length of pipe \\( (i,k) \\).\n- \\( c_p \\): specific heat capacity of water.\n- \\( T_{amb} \\): ambient temperature.\n- \\( T_{\\min}, T_{\\max} \\): minimum and maximum allowable temperatures.\n\n**Variables:**\n- \\( f_{ik} \\geq 0 \\): mass flow on edge \\( (i,k) \\).\n- \\( T_{ik}^{\\text{in}}, T_{ik}^{\\text{out}} \\): inlet and outlet temperatures on edge \\( (i,k) \\).\n- \\( x_i \\): heat supplied by supplier \\( i \\).\n- \\( Q_{ik} \\): heat delivered by pipe \\( (i,k) \\).\n- \\( s_j \\): storage state at storage node \\( j \\) (if applicable).\n\n**Optimization Problem:**\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n\\min_{f, T, x} \\quad & \\sum_{i \\in S} C_i(x_i) \\\\\n\\text{s.t.} \\quad\n& \\sum_{j: (j,i) \\in E} f_{ji} - \\sum_{k: (i,k) \\in E} f_{ik} = d_i, \\quad \\forall i \\in V \\tag{1} \\\\\n& T_{ik}^{\\text{out}} = T_{ik}^{\\text{in}} - \\alpha_{ik}(T_{ik}^{\\text{in}} - T_{amb}) L_{ik}, \\quad \\forall (i,k) \\in E \\tag{2} \\\\\n& Q_{ik} = f_{ik} c_p (T_{ik}^{\\text{in}} - T_{ik}^{\\text{out}}), \\quad \\forall (i,k) \\in E \\tag{3} \\\\\n& T_i^{\\text{out}} = \\frac{\\sum_{j: (j,i) \\in E} f_{ji} T_{ji}^{\\text{out}}}{\\sum_{j: (j,i) \\in E} f_{ji}}, \\quad \\forall i \\in M \\tag{4} \\\\\n& f_{ik} \\geq 0, \\quad T_{ik}^{\\text{in}}, T_{ik}^{\\text{out}} \\in [T_{\\min}, T_{\\max}], \\quad \\forall (i,k) \\in E \\tag{5} \\\\\n& x_i = \\sum_{k: (i,k) \\in E} Q_{ik}, \\quad \\forall i \\in S \\tag{6} \\\\\n& s_j^{\\text{out}} = s_j^{\\text{in}} + \\sum_{k: (j,k) \\in E} Q_{jk} - \\sum_{i: (i,j) \\in E} Q_{ij}, \\quad \\forall j \\in V_{stor} \\tag{7} \\\\\n& \\sum_{(i,k) \\in C^+} f_{ik} - \\sum_{(k,i) \\in C^-} f_{ki} = \\sum_{i \\in C} d_i, \\quad \\forall C \\in \\mathcal{C} \\tag{8} \\\\\n& \\text{(Symmetry, redundancy, and other constraints as detailed below)}\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\nHere, \\( C^+ \\) and \\( C^- \\) denote the forward and reverse edges in the orientation of cycle \\( C \\). For cycles with nonzero net supply/demand (\\( \\sum_{i \\in C} d_i \\neq 0 \\)), the right-hand side is set accordingly.\n\n**3. Justification of Model Simplifications**\n\nThe stationary model neglects hydraulic and transient dynamics, assumes perfect mixing at junctions, and omits pressure constraints. These simplifications are justified for operational planning horizons of 15\u201360 minutes, where hydraulic transients (governed by the momentum equation) typically settle within 10\u2013120 seconds for pipe lengths up to 5 km and flow velocities of 0.5\u20132 m/s [4,11]. Temperature dynamics are slower, but for demand variations less than 10% per hour and pipe lengths below 5 km, the temperature profile reaches steady state within 5\u201310 minutes [2,5]. Empirical studies (e.g., [2], [5], [12]) show that, for such parameter ranges, steady-state models yield dispatch solutions within 2\u20135% of full dynamic simulations in terms of cost and supply-demand balance. Pressure drops are managed by separate hydraulic controllers [6], and their impact on thermal dispatch is negligible unless the network operates near hydraulic limits or under rapid demand fluctuations. For networks with long pipes (>10 km), highly variable demand (>20% per hour), or pressure-sensitive operation, dynamic and hydraulic effects become significant and the stationary model may yield suboptimal or infeasible solutions. In such cases, our framework could be extended by incorporating dynamic mass and energy balances and explicit pressure constraints, at the cost of increased computational complexity.\n\n**4. Methodological Enhancements**\n\nWe improve"}}